Subject: virtual-io, wasix: give each thread one waker for all its waits

A wait that cannot be served at once registers its waker with whatever it
is waiting on, and both lists that hold those wakers -- an interest's slot
in virtual-io, a thread's signal subscription in wasix -- keep one entry
per distinct waker, deciding with `Waker::will_wake`. The park loop built
a parker and a waker for each wait, so no two waits were ever recognised
as the same waiter: every poll of every wait appended another entry to
every list whose source had not fired yet, and a list only drains when it
does. A source that stays quiet while the process works -- an idle
listening socket, a thread nothing signals -- therefore grew its list by
an entry per request, and the `will_wake` scan over it, which every poll
pays, grew with it. Under a single-client request loop that is quadratic:
throughput falls away steadily and the wakers are memory the process does
not get back.

Each OS thread now keeps one parker and one waker for its life and lends
them to one wait at a time, so every wait registers a waker the lists
recognise and each holds at most one entry per waiting thread. A wait
started inside the poll of another wait on the same thread still builds
its own pair, because a parker's token says only that the thread was
woken: sharing one would let the inner loop consume the token the outer
wait's event left behind.

A list that grows to 64 entries records a debug event, so that the
growth shows in a trace.

